  "response": "Resolving a sudden orchard dieback begins with a clear account of the candidate pathogen. Phytophthora cinnamomi is a soilborne oomycete that causes root and collar rot in orchard trees. Phytophthora cinnamomi is a true fungus classified in the phylum Ascomycota. Membership in the oomycete water moulds and membership in the ascomycete fungi are incompatible placements for one organism, so affirming either placement rules the other out.\n\nField diagnosis then turns on direct examination of the root system. Pathologists excavate roots at the boundary between symptomatic and asymptomatic trees to inspect for lesions. A sharply defined cinnamon-brown lesion margin in the root cambium is characteristic of Phytophthora root rot; root asphyxiation, by contrast, produces diffuse, uniform root death without a discrete advancing lesion front. Root asphyxiation is diagnosed by the presence of white mycelial fans beneath the bark at the root collar. A disorder whose signature is undifferentiated decay with no advancing front cannot be the same disorder recognised by conspicuous mycelial fans under the collar bark, so the frontless pattern excludes that criterion. After the roots have been opened up at that transition between declining and healthy trees, symptomatic root tissue is plated onto a selective medium containing pimaricin, ampicillin, rifampicin and PCNB to recover Phytophthora.\n\nThe physical setting of the affected block receives equally careful attention. Soil redox potential measurements below about 300 millivolts indicate anaerobic conditions in the root zone, a state brought about by the compacted hardpan layers that a soil pit profile reveals to be restricting vertical drainage. The dieback pattern coincided with the low-lying, poorly drained portions of the block; in other words, symptomatic trees were concentrated in the depressions of the block where water accumulated.\n\nThe laboratory outcome is what finally settles the question. Either Phytophthora was recovered from at least one of the sampled trees in the orchard, or no isolate of any Phytophthora species was obtained from the sampled trees in the orchard: one of those two outcomes must hold, and they cannot hold together. The failure of the selective plating to yield any such isolate is precisely what supports the final diagnostic report, which concluded that the altered irrigation schedule, not a pathogen, initiated the decline. A negative isolation result from well-chosen margin tissue, taken together with a decline confined to the wettest ground, leaves no pathogenic agent for the symptoms to be attributed to, and the report's conclusion follows from that evidence.",
